            "correctIndex": 1
          },
          "literalSHA256": "4a5a4eb492812889a500a3a255e8987729e5ea63f20de4c5c861a71398e8e27a",
          "primaryGround": "PRlib gives the event as 9 (22) January 1905 in Petersburg and describes troops and police firing on the peaceful workers procession (L45-L49); it names Bloody Sunday as the beginning of the First Russian Revolution (L52). The alternatives are separately documented: Decembrist uprising in December 1825 (Decembrist L44), February Revolution and overthrow of autocracy (February L59-L60), and Kronstadt garrison and sailors rising against Bolsheviks in March 1921 (Kronstadt L63). Only the key matches the prompt.",
          "locator": "Bloody Sunday L45-L52; Decembrists L44; February L59-L60; Kronstadt L63",
          "sourcePaths": [
            "docs/content-audits/question-quality-wave-8/coordinator-01a1605d/source-history-russia56/raw/prlib-bloody-sunday.json",
            "docs/content-audits/question-quality-wave-8/coordinator-01a15cdf/source-history-russia6/raw/prlib-decembrist-uprising.json",
